ROOT=Path.cwd(); ART=ROOT/"articles"; BACK=ROOT/"backup-bloco-06-artigos"; BACK.mkdir(exist_ok=True)
DATA=[{'filename': 'como-cuidar-plantas-en-verano.html', 'title': 'How to Care for Plants During Summer', 'category': 'INDOOR PLANTS', 'meta': 'Learn how to care for plants during summer by adjusting watering, sunlight, temperature, ventilation, and routines for heat waves and vacations.', 'intro': 'Summer brings longer days, higher temperatures, and faster evaporation, so plants need careful observation to adjust watering, sunlight, ventilation, and protection without overdoing any one task.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2><ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Water Properly During Hot Days</li><li>Protect Plants From Excessive Sun</li><li>Control Humidity and Temperature</li><li>Ventilation and Problem Prevention</li><li>Practical Routine for Vacations and Heat Waves</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Summer brings longer days, higher temperatures, and faster evaporation, so plants often need closer observation. The goal is not simply to water more often. Good summer care means adjusting watering, sunlight, ventilation, and protection according to each species and the real conditions around it.</p>\n<p>Before changing a routine, identify the plant, its container, the type of growing medium, and how much direct light it receives. A plant on a windy balcony can dry much faster than the same species in a sheltered room. Likewise, a small pot can lose moisture much sooner than a large container.</p>\n<h2>Water Properly During Hot Days</h2>\n<p>Heat can dry the growing medium quickly, but not every plant needs daily watering. Check moisture before adding water. For suitable plants, feeling the upper layer of the growing medium, observing the weight of the pot, and knowing the plant's normal appearance can be more useful than following a rigid calendar.</p>\n<p>When watering is necessary, water thoroughly enough to moisten the root ball and allow excess water to leave through drainage holes where appropriate. Empty water that remains in saucers after drainage. Repeated shallow watering may wet only the surface while deeper roots remain dry.</p>\n<p>Early morning is often a practical time to water outdoor plants because temperatures are milder and plants have time to take up moisture before the hottest part of the day. Small containers, hanging baskets, and windy balconies deserve more frequent checks because they can dry rapidly.</p>\n<p>Mulch can help reduce evaporation in garden beds and larger containers when it is suitable for the plants. Keep mulch away from sensitive stems and crowns, and do not use it to hide persistently saturated soil.</p>\n<h3>Recognize Signs Without Guessing</h3>\n<p>Wilting during a hot afternoon does not always mean the soil is dry. Some plants temporarily wilt under intense heat even when moisture is available. Check the growing medium before watering again. Yellowing, soft growth, or persistently wet soil can indicate that excess water is part of the problem.</p>\n<p>Make one change at a time whenever possible. If you simultaneously move the plant, change watering, fertilize, and alter sunlight exposure, it becomes difficult to understand what produced an improvement or a new problem.</p>\n<h2>Protect Plants From Excessive Sun</h2>\n<p>A plant that enjoys bright light does not necessarily tolerate intense direct midday sun. Leaves can scorch when a plant accustomed to indoor light is suddenly moved to a sunny terrace. Increase exposure gradually when moving plants into stronger light.</p>\n<p>Indoors, a light curtain can soften an extremely bright window. Observe the path of the sun throughout the season. A window that felt gentle in spring may receive stronger radiation during summer, depending on orientation and location.</p>\n<p>Pale, dry, or brown patches on the most exposed leaves can be associated with sun damage, although other causes should also be considered. Avoid assuming that every brown mark has the same cause.</p>\n<p>Newly transplanted garden plants can be especially sensitive while roots establish. Temporary shade and careful moisture monitoring may reduce stress during very hot periods. Avoid unnecessary heavy pruning during extreme heat unless pruning is needed for plant health or safety.</p>\n<h2>Control Humidity and Temperature</h2>\n<p>High temperatures increase evaporation and change the microclimate around plants. Some tropical species appreciate moderate atmospheric humidity, but keeping leaves and soil constantly wet is not a universal solution. Excess moisture combined with poor air circulation can encourage fungal problems.</p>\n<p>Move sensitive indoor plants away from hot air outlets and surfaces that become extremely warm. On balconies, dark containers can heat considerably in direct sun, which can affect roots. Providing shade to the container while preserving the light needs of the foliage can sometimes help.</p>\n<p>Do not assume that misting is required for every houseplant. Its usefulness depends on the species and environment, and frequent wet foliage can be undesirable for some plants. Use species-specific guidance instead of applying the same humidity routine to the entire collection.</p>\n<h2>Ventilation and Problem Prevention</h2>\n<p>Good air movement can help prevent stagnant, excessively humid conditions, especially where many plants are grouped together. Indoors, ventilate reasonably while avoiding extreme hot drafts from appliances or air that is excessively dry from cooling systems.</p>\n<p>Inspect leaves, stems, and the surface of the growing medium regularly. Warm conditions can accelerate some pest populations. Early detection is easier to manage than a severe infestation. Isolate a badly affected plant when appropriate and identify the problem before choosing a treatment.</p>\n<p>Keep tools and containers clean. Remove dead plant material when appropriate, because accumulated debris can hold moisture and hide pests. Do not apply pesticides, fertilizers, or homemade mixtures automatically during heat stress; first determine whether they are necessary and suitable.</p>\n<h3>Fertilizing During Summer</h3>\n<p>Some actively growing plants may use fertilizer during summer, while others may slow down under extreme heat. Follow species and product recommendations. Applying extra fertilizer to a stressed or very dry plant does not solve heat stress and can create additional problems.</p>\n<h2>Practical Routine for Vacations and Heat Waves</h2>\n<p>Before leaving home, group plants according to their actual needs rather than placing every pot in the same conditions. Water those that need it, move especially vulnerable containers away from harsh temporary exposure, and make sure drainage is working.</p>\n<p>If someone will care for the plants, leave simple written instructions. Indicate which plants should be checked frequently and which should be allowed to dry more between waterings. Clear instructions reduce the risk of every pot receiving the same amount of water.</p>\n<p>During a heat wave, check vulnerable plants more often, particularly small pots and newly planted specimens. Avoid unnecessary repotting, major pruning, or other stressful work during the hottest days unless there is an urgent reason.</p>\n<p>After temperatures moderate, review the collection. Remove only tissue that is clearly damaged when appropriate, restore the normal routine gradually, and avoid compensating for a hot week with excessive watering or fertilizer.</p>\n<h2>Summer Care for Different Growing Situations</h2>\n<p>Indoor plants near windows can experience strong localized heat even when the room feels comfortable. Touching the pot and observing leaf temperature indirectly can alert you to unusually hot conditions. Move delicate foliage back from glass when necessary.</p>\n<p>Outdoor containers require attention to both foliage and roots. A plant may tolerate full sun while its root system suffers when the container overheats. Light-colored outer pots, strategic shading of the container, or relocation during extreme conditions may help when appropriate.</p>\n<p>Plants growing directly in garden soil often have access to a larger moisture reserve than containers, but newly planted specimens still require monitoring until their roots establish. Deep, appropriately spaced watering generally encourages a more resilient root zone than frequent superficial wetting.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Should I water plants every day in summer?</h3><p>No. Watering frequency depends on species, pot size, soil, temperature, wind, and light. Check actual moisture rather than relying only on the calendar.</p>\n<h3>Can I move indoor plants outside for summer?</h3><p>Some plants can benefit, but the transition should be gradual and the outdoor conditions must suit the species. Sudden exposure to strong sun can damage leaves.</p>\n<h3>Is midday watering harmful?</h3><p>If a plant is severely dry, it should not be left without needed water simply because it is midday. Early morning is often more efficient for routine outdoor watering, but actual plant needs come first.</p>\n<h3>Should I fertilize during a heat wave?</h3><p>Do not fertilize automatically. Follow the needs of the species and product instructions, and avoid adding fertilizer as a supposed treatment for heat stress.</p>\n<h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Observe Seasonal Changes</h2><p>Summer conditions are not identical from week to week. A rainy period can reduce watering needs, while dry wind can increase them rapidly. Review the routine whenever weather patterns change rather than assuming that a schedule established in early summer will remain correct until autumn.</p><p>Keep brief notes for plants that are difficult to read. Recording watering dates, changes in location, and visible responses can reveal useful patterns and make future summer care more predictable.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Successful summer plant care is based on observation and adjustment. Check moisture before watering, protect sensitive plants from excessive sun, maintain suitable ventilation, and monitor containers that heat or dry rapidly.</p>\n<p>There is no single summer schedule for every plant. By respecting species-specific needs and making gradual changes, you can help indoor and outdoor plants remain healthier through hot days, vacations, and periods of intense sunlight.</p>\n"}, {'filename': 'como-cuidar-superficies-de-piedra-natural.html', 'title': 'How to Care for Natural Stone Surfaces', 'category': 'CLEANING & MAINTENANCE', 'meta': 'Learn how to care for natural stone surfaces with safe cleaning, stain prevention, moisture control, sealing guidance, and protection for high-use areas.', 'intro': 'Natural stone can remain attractive for many years when cleaning, moisture control, stain prevention, and maintenance are adapted to the specific stone and finish.', 'body': "\n<h2>In This Article</h2><ul><li>Introduction</li><li>Identify the Type and Finish of the Stone</li><li>Clean Natural Stone Safely</li><li>Prevent Stains and Moisture Damage</li><li>Protect High-Use Surfaces</li><li>Maintain Sealed and Unsealed Stone</li><li>Common Mistakes to Avoid</li><li>Frequently Asked Questions</li><li>Conclusion</li></ul>\n<h2>Introduction</h2>\n<p>Natural stone can be durable and visually distinctive, but marble, granite, limestone, travertine, slate, and other stones do not all react in the same way to cleaners, moisture, acids, abrasion, or sealers. Correct care begins by identifying the material and its finish before applying a product.</p>\n<p>A polished kitchen countertop, a honed bathroom floor, and an exterior stone surface may require different routines even when the stone itself is similar. Manufacturer, installer, or stone-care instructions should take priority over generic advice.</p>\n<h2>Identify the Type and Finish of the Stone</h2>\n<p>Before deep cleaning or stain treatment, confirm what stone you have. Marble and many limestones are sensitive to acids, while other stones may tolerate different products. The surface finish also matters: polished, honed, textured, and natural-cleft finishes can respond differently to cleaning and wear.</p>\n<p>If the material is unknown, avoid aggressive experiments. Start with the least invasive method and test any new product in an inconspicuous area. For valuable installations or serious staining, a stone-care professional can identify the material and recommend an appropriate treatment.</p>\n<p>Keep documentation from the installer when available. Information about the stone, grout, sealer, and recommended maintenance can save time later and prevent incompatible products from being used.</p>\n<h2>Clean Natural Stone Safely</h2>\n<p>Routine cleaning should remove loose dust and grit before wet cleaning. Fine particles can act as abrasives under shoes, mops, or cloths. Sweep, vacuum with a suitable floor attachment, or dust the surface using a method appropriate for the installation.</p>\n<p>For wet cleaning, use a cleaner specifically compatible with the stone and follow its dilution instructions. A pH-neutral stone cleaner is commonly appropriate for many installations, but always check the product and stone requirements.</p>\n<p>Avoid assuming that vinegar, lemon, or other acidic household products are safe because they are natural. Acids can etch calcite-based stones such as marble and limestone, changing the finish in a way that ordinary cleaning cannot reverse.</p>\n<p>Use soft cloths, nonabrasive pads, or suitable mops. Steel wool, harsh scouring powders, and aggressive brushes can scratch or dull some surfaces. Rinse residues when the product requires it and dry areas where standing water could create marks.</p>\n<h3>Daily and Weekly Cleaning</h3>\n<p>On countertops, remove crumbs and spills promptly. On floors, control tracked-in grit with clean entrance mats and regular dust removal. In bathrooms, wiping excess water from stone after use can reduce mineral deposits and soap residue.</p>\n<p>Do not mix cleaning chemicals. Combining products can create dangerous fumes or damage stone, grout, sealers, and nearby materials. Use one compatible product according to its label.</p>\n<h2>Prevent Stains and Moisture Damage</h2>\n<p>Porosity varies between stones and finishes. Some surfaces absorb liquids more readily than others. Blot spills rather than spreading them, especially oils, wine, coffee, cosmetics, and strongly colored liquids.</p>\n<p>Coasters, trays, trivets, and placemats can protect countertops and tables from spills, heat, and scratching. Under toiletries or plant pots, use suitable protection so moisture does not remain trapped against the stone.</p>\n<p>In showers and other wet areas, maintenance includes more than the stone itself. Grout, joints, drainage, ventilation, and waterproofing all affect performance. Persistent dampness or leaks should be investigated rather than repeatedly covered with cleaning products.</p>\n<p>If a stain penetrates the stone, identify the type of stain before attempting removal. Oil, organic material, rust, and mineral deposits can require different treatments. An unsuitable stain remover may worsen the appearance.</p>\n<h2>Protect High-Use Surfaces</h2>\n<p>Kitchen stone receives frequent contact with food, cookware, oils, and cleaning products. Wipe spills quickly and use cutting boards instead of cutting directly on the surface. A cutting board protects both the stone and knife edges and helps maintain a hygienic work area.</p>\n<p>Use trivets or heat-resistant supports under very hot cookware. Some stone may tolerate heat well, but thermal shock, resin-filled areas, sealers, or adjacent materials can react differently. Protection is a simple preventive habit.</p>\n<p>On floors, furniture pads can reduce scratching when chairs and tables move. Keep pads clean because trapped grit can itself become abrasive. Lift heavy furniture rather than dragging it across the stone.</p>\n<p>Exterior stone faces weather, dirt, organic growth, and freeze-thaw conditions in some climates. Use cleaning and sealing methods specifically intended for outdoor stone and local conditions.</p>\n<h2>Maintain Sealed and Unsealed Stone</h2>\n<p>Not every natural stone surface requires the same sealer, and sealing does not make stone completely stain-proof or maintenance-free. Some dense stones may need little or no penetrating sealer, while more absorbent materials may benefit from one.</p>\n<p>If the surface was sealed, follow the installer or sealer manufacturer's guidance for inspection and reapplication. Do not reseal simply because a fixed amount of time has passed without considering the product, stone, wear, and performance.</p>\n<p>Applying incompatible sealers or repeated unnecessary coats can create haze, uneven appearance, or residue. Existing coatings may also affect which products can be used later.</p>\n<p>Grout and joints should be inspected separately. Cracked grout, failed caulk, or movement joints can allow moisture to reach areas that surface cleaning cannot address. Repair methods depend on the installation.</p>\n<h2>Deal With Etching, Scratches, and Wear</h2>\n<p>Etching is different from a stain. On acid-sensitive stone, an acidic substance can chemically alter the surface and leave a dull or lighter mark. Cleaning alone will not necessarily restore the original polish because the finish itself has changed.</p>\n<p>Small scratches or worn traffic paths may require professional honing or polishing, particularly on large polished floors or valuable countertops. Trying to correct them with abrasive household products can enlarge the affected area.</p>\n<p>Photograph damage before attempting treatment. This helps you compare results and gives a professional useful information if specialized restoration becomes necessary.</p>\n<h2>Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>\n<p>Do not use vinegar or lemon juice as universal stone cleaners. Do not scrub every stain aggressively, and do not assume bleach or other strong chemicals are suitable for the stone or sealer.</p>\n<p>Avoid flooding floors or leaving water standing for long periods. Excess moisture can affect grout, subfloors, edges, or adjacent materials even when the stone itself seems resistant.</p>\n<p>Do not apply waxes, polishes, or sealers simply to make the stone look shinier. Products that are not designed for the installation can create slippery surfaces, residue, or uneven appearance.</p>\n<p>Finally, avoid treating all natural stone as granite. Different mineral compositions mean different sensitivities. Identification is the foundation of safe maintenance.</p>\n<h2>Create a Simple Maintenance Schedule</h2>\n<p>Daily care can be limited to removing grit and wiping spills. Weekly or periodic cleaning can use the appropriate stone-safe product. Every few months, inspect grout, joints, high-traffic areas, and locations where water regularly collects.</p>\n<p>Once or twice a year, review whether the surface has changed in appearance or performance. This does not mean resealing automatically; it means checking for wear, staining, damaged joints, or other conditions that deserve attention.</p>\n<p>Keep the names of products that have worked safely. A simple record prevents accidental switching to an incompatible cleaner and makes future maintenance easier for anyone else caring for the home.</p>\n<h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>\n<h3>Can I clean natural stone with vinegar?</h3><p>Vinegar is acidic and can etch marble, limestone, and other acid-sensitive stones. Use a cleaner confirmed to be compatible with your specific stone.</p>\n<h3>Does all natural stone need sealing?</h3><p>No. Sealing needs depend on the stone, finish, location, and existing treatment. Follow installer, manufacturer, or qualified stone-care guidance.</p>\n<h3>How should I clean a spill?</h3><p>Blot it promptly without spreading it, then clean with a product suitable for the stone and the type of spill.</p>\n<h3>Can scratches be polished out at home?</h3><p>Minor marks vary greatly by stone and finish. On polished or valuable surfaces, professional restoration is often safer than experimenting with abrasives.</p>\n<h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Protect Stone During Projects and Renovations</h2><p>When painting, repairing, or moving furniture, cover stone with a protective material that will not trap damaging moisture or scratch the finish. Do not tape directly to delicate stone unless the adhesive is confirmed safe.</p><p>Construction dust can be abrasive, so remove it carefully before normal mopping. If cement, grout, paint, or another building material reaches the stone, identify both substances before selecting a remover.</p><h2>Conclusion</h2>\n<p>Natural stone care is easiest when you know the material and use compatible products. Remove grit, clean gently, blot spills promptly, protect high-use areas, and pay attention to grout, joints, and moisture.</p>\n<p>Avoid universal household remedies and unnecessary sealing. When stains, etching, scratches, or structural moisture problems exceed routine maintenance, professional assessment can protect the surface from further damage and preserve its appearance for years.</p>\n"}]
